ts, rather than just listing what's done. Let me trace back through the releases to find which ones actually have unstarted use cases paired with ready SRD requirements. Looking at the ready requirements more carefully, I see that srd075 through srd078 actually have some requirements marked as ready. Let me reconsider what this means — these SRDs have partial readiness with specific requirements like R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 flagged, though earlier requirement groups aren't listed, which could mean they're already complete or still in progress.\n\nChecking the git history shows that b2sum (srd076) has had recent work completing R1.1 through R1.4 and R2.1 through R2.3 plus R3.1, leaving R3.2, R3.3, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 as the remaining ready requirements.\n\nFor sha384sum (srd075), the ready requirements are R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3, meaning earlier requirements were already completed. Looking at the requirement states, I need to focus on which requirements actually have \"ready\" status and propose tasks for the earliest release that has ready work available. Release 09.0 has the most ready requirements across multiple tools: sha384sum with 4 ready, b2sum with 5 ready, cksum with 10 ready, and sum with at least 6 ready.\n\nSince I can't access the requirements.yaml file to check weights, I'll work with the default weight of 1 for each requirement as specified in the constraints. I need to identify which release is earliest and has incomplete work to focus on.\n\nLooking at the requirement states across releases, 09.0 appears to be the earliest with unstarted code and ready requirements—sha384sum has 4 ready requirements, b2sum has 5, and cksum has 10, while sha224sum seems to already be complete or in progress.\n\nNow I'm planning how to distribute these requirements across tasks with a 4-task limit, keeping each task to at most 4 sub-requirements. The sha384sum requirements fit neatly into one task, but b2sum's 5 requirements need to be split across two tasks t