daries, so I need to reconsider whether that's acceptable or if I should restructure these tasks.\n\nThe batch constraint limits me to 4 tasks maximum, so I need to prioritize the highest-impact work. Let me check if I can consolidate: adding b2sum R4.3 to the existing b2sum task would give me 5 SRD requirements total, which exceeds the per-task limit. Since sha384sum already maxes out at 4 requirements, I can't shift anything there either.\n\nThe better approach is to redistribute b2sum's requirements across two tasks—keeping R3.2, R3.3, and R4.1 together in one task, then pairing R4.2 with another utility to avoid leaving it isolated.\n\nLooking at the requirement patterns, R3.x likely covers verification and check modes while R4.x handles error handling and edge cases. This suggests a natural grouping where check-mode requirements stay together, separate from error-handling ones. So I'll keep b2sum's check-mode requirements (R3.2, R3.3) with R4.1, then handle R4.2 and R4.3 in a follow-up task. The weight calculations check out for all four tasks, each staying within their limits. I realize I can't actually read the SRD files directly, but the project context does provide the roadmap, architecture details, and requirement states—I should work with what's available there rather than trying to access files I can't reach.\n\nSince the stitch agent will read the SRDs themselves, I can structure the task descriptions to reference specific requirement IDs and let the agent handle the implementation details. The key is pointing them to the right SRD files and requirement numbers so they understand what needs to be built.\n\nFor sha384sum, I need to note that earlier requirements are already done, so the stitch agent should focus on R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 in the cmd/sha384sum/main file.\n\ngo and test file. I'll reference the SRD spec, existing implementation, and the shared hash library for context.\n\nFor b2sum, the remaining work splits into two tasks: R3.2, R3.3, 