traints—Task 0 with srd075 requirements adds up to 4, and Task 1 with srd076 requirements is also tracking within limits.\n\nHowever, I'm noticing a tension between the \"Do NOT use any tools\" constraint and needing to know the file paths for existing implementations. From the git history, tasks 5493 and 5495 were for b2sum, so cmd/b2sum/ should already exist with its main and test files. Similarly, sha384sum was partially implemented in earlier requirements, just not fully listed here.\n\nLooking more carefully at the requirement states for srd075-sha384sum, only R3.2 through R4.3 are marked as ready, which means R1.x, R2.x, and R3.1 were already completed. So cmd/sha384sum/ exists with partial implementation. For srd077-cksum and srd078-sum, all requirements are listed as ready, meaning nothing's been done yet and I need to create those directories from scratch.\n\nSo my tasks are: first, stitch together the remaining sha384sum requirements (R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3) by modifying the existing main.go and test file. Then handle b2sum in two parts—first the R3.2, R3.3, R4.1, R4.2 requirements building on previous work, then the final R4.3 requirement. After that comes the cmd/cksum/ and cmd/sum/ implementations.\n\nBut I'm second-guessing whether the contract task requirement applies to cmd/ packages. These are command-line tools with main() functions, not libraries with exported types and interfaces. The constraint about defining exported types and function signatures as stubs seems designed for library packages, not executables. Let me think through whether I actually need to propose a contract task for cksum before the implementation tasks. I need to reconsider the ordering based on dependencies rather than just alphabetical order. Task 1 depends on task 0 since they're both part of b2sum, while tasks 2 and 3 are independent. So the sequence should be: b2sum tasks first (0 then 1), followed by cksum and sha384sum in alphabetical order.\n\nNow I'm working on the task descriptions themselves—they need to be self-contained since the execution system will only see each description in isolation without the broader context.\n\nFor each utility, I need to specify the deliverable type as code, pull in the relevant SRD documentation and existing source files along with the shared hash utilities, list out which files get created or modified, map the SRD requirements to specific task requirements, document the architectural decisions from the SRD, and define what success looks like through testable acceptance criteria.\n\nLooking at what I know about these three utilities: sha384sum is part of the SHA-2 family that shares infrastructure through pkg/hashutil and uses gsha384sum as the reference; b2sum handles BLAKE2b with variable-length digest support, also leveraging pkg/hashutil with gb2sum as reference; and cksum implements POSIX CRC-32 checksums.\n\nI'm mapping out which requirements each utility still needs to complete—b2sum has R3.2 through R4.3 remaining, cksum starts fresh with R1.1 through R1.4, and sha384sum needs R3.2 through R4.3 to finish. The stitch agent will have the full SRD text, so I just need to point it to the right files and scope rather than repeating the actual requirement language.\n\nBut I need to make sure each task is completely self-contained since the agent won't have access to my analysis or the existing issues. The planning constitution says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, though the existing git history shows them combined—I should verify whether that constraint applies here or if the four-requirement-per-task limit takes precedence. of the implementation. It's not a separate unit test suite—it's the verification mechanism. So combining them makes sense here, especially since the existing successful tasks followed this pattern.\n\nNow I need to write out the YAML with precise formatting, making sure to include the weight budget arithmetic as requested. I should also map the requirements to SRD IDs and let the stitch agent handle reading the full details, but I'll write meaningful task-level requirements that connect to those identifiers. I need to be careful here—I shouldn't invent requirement descriptions when I don't have the actual SRD text. The stitch agent will look up what R3.2 means, so I should just reference it directly rather than guessing at the details. I also need to track the weight budget for each task, assuming a default weight of 1 per requirement unless I have the actual weights from requirements.yaml, and verify that the total doesn't exceed 4 before proposing anything.\n\nLooking at the requirement states for srd075-sha384sum, I notice that only R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 are listed as ready, which means the earlier requirements either have already been completed or aren't dependencies for this task. Let me focus on what's actually marked as ready rather than making assumptions about the full requirement chain.\n\nFor srd077-cksum and srd078-sum, all requirements across R1.1 through R3.3 are marked as ready, indicating these are starting from scratch. This means I'll be creating new files for cksum and sum, while modifying existing files for sha384sum and b2sum.\n\nSince cmd/cksum is a command package with only a main() function and no exported types, a contract task would be unnecessary. I'm ordering the tasks canonically: b2sum first, then cksum as the new package, then sha384sum, with lower-numbered requirements taking precedence within each package.\n\nNow I need to verify the weight budget—each requirement has a default weight of 1, but some might be higher, which could affect my grouping. In previous releases, complex weight-4 requirements were split into separate groups in the SRDs.\n\nLooking at the requirement states, I should only propose work for requirements marked as \"ready.\" Checking the releases in order, I see that releases before 09.0 don't have any actionable SRDs in the requirement_states field, meani